<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Treaty of San Ildefonso</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1777">1777</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Treaty of San Ildefonso (1777) · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-portuguese-war-1776-1777">Spanish–Portuguese War (1776–1777)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Treaty of San Ildefonso was signed on 1 October 1777 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Portugal at the royal palace of La Granja de San Ildefonso, ending their war over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>. Portugal gave up Colonia del Sacramento and the Banda Oriental to Spain, which returned the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-catarina">Santa Catarina</a>. The treaty also defined much of the border between their South American empires.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-treaty-of-san-ildefonso.jpg" alt="First Treaty of San Ildefonso" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nagihuin · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imperios_Espa%C3%B1ol_y_Portugu%C3%A9s_1790.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>For nearly 300 years differing readings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a> had set <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Portugal at odds over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>, which Portugal tried to annex to its Brazilian colonies. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-madrid-1750">Treaty of Madrid</a> of 1750 was annulled in 1761 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles III of Spain</a>. During the Fantastic War of 1762-1763 Spain took Colonia del Sacramento and much of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande-do-sul">Rio Grande do Sul</a>, but the Treaty of Paris (1763) made it return Colonia, and by 1777 Portugal had reoccupied Rio Grande do Sul.</p><p>Portuguese encroachments let merchants evade the rule that trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a> pass through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> became a centre of smuggling. To regain control <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> created the Viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> in 1776, with its capital at Buenos Aires. An undeclared war grew bitter from 1775 and formally began in 1776. In February 1777 the new viceroy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-cevallos">Pedro de Cevallos</a>, led an expedition of 116 ships and 19,000 troops, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-catarina">Santa Catarina</a> and forced Colonia to surrender in July. In August he learned that Joseph I of Portugal had died in February; his daughter Maria I sued for peace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Besides Colonia del Sacramento, Portugal ceded the nearby island of San Gabriel, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> acknowledged Portuguese control of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. A boundary commission was set up to mark out the colonial borders, later confirmed by the Treaty of El Pardo (1778). Portugal also agreed to prevent smuggling and to close its ports to military or merchant ships of nations hostile to Spain, a clause aimed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, with which Spain was at war from 1779 to 1783.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Charles III hoped a settled border would help the new viceroyalty grow and calm its people. The result was partial: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> restricted trade with Spain and brought high tariffs and taxes, smuggling stayed profitable, and heavy levies fed unrest such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolt-of-the-comuneros-new-granada">Revolt of the Comuneros</a> of 1781 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>. After Trafalgar in 1805 cut Spain's links with its colonies, local forces repulsed British attacks on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> in 1806 and 1807, and the viceroyalty was dissolved during the Argentine War of Independence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Treaty of San Ildefonso” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pedro de Cevallos captured Colonia for the last time in 1777, and the <b>Treaty of San Ildefonso</b> that year confirmed Spanish possession.</blockquote><a href="/state/colonia-del-sacramento">Colonia del Sacramento</a></li></ul></section>
